---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RentVsBuyCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/RentVsBuyCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="rent-vs-buy-calculator">
  <RentVsBuyCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the rent vs buy calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The rent vs buy calculator follows two households month by month. Both start with the same cash: the
      buyer spends it on the down payment and closing costs, and the renter invests it. Each month the
      buyer pays the mortgage, property tax, home insurance, maintenance and any HOA fee, and the renter
      pays rent and renter's insurance. The one who pays less invests the difference.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mortgage payment is <code>M = L × i ÷ (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code>, where <code>L</code> is the loan,
      <code>i</code> is the yearly rate divided by 12 and <code>n</code> is the number of months.
      Investments grow at <code>j = (1 + R)^(1/12) − 1</code> a month, where <code>R</code> is the
      investment return. The home value grows at the home price growth rate. At the end of each year the
      buyer's wealth is <code>home value × (1 − selling costs) − loan balance + investments</code>, and the
      renter's wealth is the renter's investments. The break-even year is the first year in which the
      buyer's wealth is the larger one.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 400,000 home with 20% down at 6.5% for 30 years has a payment of 2,022.62 a month. With 3% closing
      costs, both households start with 92,000. In month 1 owning costs 2,872.62 and renting 2,215.00, so
      the renter invests 657.62. With 3% home price growth, 3% yearly rent increases and a 6% investment
      return, after 10 years the buyer has 234,029 and the renter 241,598: renting is ahead by 7,569.
      Buying gets ahead in year 12.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Taxes are left out: the mortgage interest deduction, tax on the gain from the home and tax on
      investment returns. Growth rates and the investment return stay the same every year. Property tax and
      maintenance follow the home value; insurance and the HOA fee stay fixed. The result assumes you sell
      at the end of the year you choose.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
